<description>
Drop the singleton marker into the matching red U-shaped container, or make a bottom-row puddle if no closed container is hit.

Input structure
- The grid has a dominant background, one singleton marker pixel on the top row,
  and one other foreground color forming vertical bars and bottom segments.
- A closed container is a U shape: two vertical side bars with a continuous
  bottom segment between them and an empty rectangular interior above the bottom.

Rule
- Remove the top marker from its original cell.
- If exactly one closed U-shaped container has the marker column within its
  horizontal footprint, fill that container's empty interior with the marker color.
- If no closed container is under the marker column, fill the whole bottom row
  with the marker color.

Key insight
- The marker does not identify a color by value; it identifies a vertical drop
  column, and only a structurally closed U shape can catch and contain the fill.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="30f42897" lines="51-66">
<description>
Repeat the single colored border segment around the rectangular border, leaving one segment-length gap between copies.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- There is exactly one foreground color.
- All foreground pixels form one contiguous segment on the rectangular border, possibly crossing the cycle start.

Rule:
- Unwrap the grid border clockwise into a cyclic path.
- Let n be the length of the foreground segment.
- Copy the segment with the same color every 2*n border positions around the cycle.

Key insight:
- The foreground segment is not extended by rows or columns directly; it is a motif on the perimeter cycle, repeated with an equal-sized gap.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="eval" puzzle_id="cbebaa4b" lines="51-65">
<description>
Assemble scattered jigsaw sprites around a central yellow anchor by pairing red connector pixels.

Input structure:
- bg = black (0). Several colored sprites scattered on the grid.
- One anchor sprite contains a 3×3 yellow (4) block.
- Each sprite has red (2) connector pixels along its edges.

Rule:
- The anchor stays in place; remaining sprites attach one at a time.
- Each sprite is translated so that one of its red pixels overlaps an unpaired red pixel on the growing assembly.
- Every red connector must pair with exactly one other, forming a complete joined structure.

Key insight: Red pixels act as jigsaw pegs — each must match exactly one counterpart, and the assembly order is determined by which unpaired connectors remain.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="ac6f9922" lines="18-40">
<description>
Summarize the enclosed table slots by the single object color inside each slot, using the slot background color for empty slots.

Input structure
- The most common color forms the outer background and all enclosed slot
  backgrounds.
- The enclosed background components form a complete rectangular array of
  slots, ordered by their positions.
- Each slot contains either no other color or exactly one object color.

Rule
- Find all 4-connected components of the most common color that do not touch
  the input edge.
- Sort their bounding boxes into row and column order to define the output
  table.
- For each slot, output its unique non-background color if present; otherwise
  output the background color.

Key insight
- The large grid is an expanded table: the enclosed background components are
  the cells, and any colored object drawn inside a cell is that cell's summary
  value.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="d749d46f" lines="32-51">
<description>
Pack each solid foreground rectangle twice: wide across the top and tall from the bottom.

Input structure
The grid has one background color and one foreground color; the foreground forms
separate solid rectangles, ordered from left to right.

Rule
Build a 10-row background canvas whose width is the packed width of the wide
versions of the rectangles. For each source rectangle, paint a top-aligned
rectangle of shape min(h,w) by max(h,w), with one background column between
consecutive rectangles. Then, starting again from the left edge, paint a
bottom-aligned rectangle of shape max(h,w) by min(h,w) for each source rectangle,
again separated by one background column.

Key insight
The input rectangles are not copied in their original orientations; each object
contributes one horizontal-facing and one vertical-facing normalized rectangle,
preserving left-to-right object order.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="train" puzzle_id="412b6263" lines="39-54">
<description>
Infer the background, rotate the whole input 90 degrees counterclockwise, and stack two framed copies vertically.

Input structure:
- Blue is absent from the input.
- One rare marker color appears in exactly two pixels.
- The background is the unique non-marker color whose removal leaves exactly two 4-connected foreground objects, each with one marker pixel and one body color.
- The whole grid, including background and foreground objects, is the sprite to transform.

Rule:
- Rotate the entire input grid 90 degrees counterclockwise.
- Create a blue one-pixel frame around two vertical copies of the rotated grid.
- Use the input background color for the two endpoints of every horizontal frame/separator row.

Key insight: The output is not a crop of the foreground objects; it is two copies of the complete rotated input, separated and enclosed by a newly added blue border.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

